thread:
anya: quick heads-up — hitting a nasty edge with SSE + gzip on our JS client. we decompress chunks as they arrive, but when we refresh auth mid-stream we reopen the connection and sometimes a partial JSON object from the previous stream gets concatenated with the first chunk of the new stream. parser (incremental JSON decoder) throws and the client starts piling up buffered chunks -> memory spike. anyone seen this pattern?

matt: ouch. are you doing streaming decompression (inflate incremental) or waiting until a whole blob?

anya: streaming inflate. we use pako/fflate streaming API to decode bytes progressively, then feed text pieces to an incremental JSON parser. the reconnect path reuses the same parser instance for speed which seems wrong.

sam: reusing parser instance across connection restarts sounds risky. best to reset parser state on any reconnect. but also need to make backpressure so the socket doesn't keep filling node buffers while parser is stuck retrying.

lee: two practical moves: 1) on reconnect, flush any partial parse buffer as an "incomplete fragment" metric and drop it (log it), then restart parser; 2) ensure stream read loop checks a bounded queue size and pauses the underlying socket/reader when queue > N.

anya: yeah plan A: reset parser on reconnect. curious about the pause/resume part — example?

matt: Node fetch + streams example (very minimal):

```js
const reader = res.body.getReader();
let queueSize = 0;
const MAX_QUEUE = 32; // number of chunks
async function pump() {
  while (true) {
    const { value, done } = await reader.read();
    if (done) break;
    queue.push(value); queueSize++;
    if (queueSize > MAX_QUEUE) {
      // pause underlying source by not calling read until queue drains
      await waitUntilQueueUnder(MAX_QUEUE / 2);
    }
    processQueueItem();
  }
}
```

This pattern gives you backpressure at the reader layer by controlling when you call read(). for low-level sockets you'd call stream.pause()/resume().

sam: also consider this for the incremental JSON parser: keep a small buffer for the current open object (like 4KB). if it exceeds X, treat as parse-failure and discard. that avoids unbounded memory if malformed/garbled fragments arrive.

anya: good call. question about gzip/inflate order — should we re-init the inflator on reconnect too? we're seeing leftover internal state in pako that can cause garbled output sometimes.

lee: yes — re-init inflator. streaming decompressors keep sliding windows that are not valid across separate streams. always create a fresh inflator for a new connection.

ci-bot: deploy-checklist: create an issue in repo client-js with labels: sse, streaming, backpressure, bug. assign to @anya.

anya: will open the issue with repro and a small heap trace. plan: 1) reset inflator + parser on reconnect, 2) implement bounded queue + reader pause/resume, 3) metric for "discarded partial objects" so we can assess impact, 4) add integration test that simulates mid-stream auth refresh.

matt: add a test that deliberately splits JSON tokens across reconnect boundary — that's the real regression check.

sam: FYI if anyone needs to repro quickly: I have a small script to mux two SSE streams with a short gap to force reconnect and that reproduces the concat. can paste if helpful.

anya: pls paste it, would help. thanks all — will follow up in the issue with PR link. :thumbsup:
